Chapter: If Let Join

 A `let` bound to an `if` must end its then-branch with a jump over the
 else-branch. The emitter may skip that jump when the then-branch has
 already transferred control, and it used to decide that by reading the
 byte five back from the end of the emitted code and asking whether it
 was 0xE9. That is the opcode of a five-byte `jmp rel32`, but it is also
 the ModRM byte of `add rcx, r13` (`4c 01 e9`), so a then-branch ending
 in that add followed by a four-byte store looked like a jump, the join
 was omitted, and the then-branch fell through into the else -- which
 overwrote the result it had just computed. Both arms then produced the
 else value.

 Whether the byte lands five back depends on the surrounding encoding,
 which is why the arity sweep below matters: at seven parameters the
 local spills and the store is four bytes, at six and eight it is not.
 Each function returns 5 when the join is emitted and 999 when it is
 not.

Section: Arity Sweep

  w6 : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  w6 (op) (x) (y) (p4) (p5) (p6) =
   let c = if op == 1 then x + y else 999
   in c

  w7 : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  w7 (op) (x) (y) (p4) (p5) (p6) (p7) =
   let c = if op == 1 then x + y else 999
   in c

  w8 : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  w8 (op) (x) (y) (p4) (p5) (p6) (p7) (p8) =
   let c = if op == 1 then x + y else 999
   in c

  w9 : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  w9 (op) (x) (y) (p4) (p5) (p6) (p7) (p8) (p9) =
   let c = if op == 1 then x + y else 999
   in c

Section: Other Then-Branch Shapes

  s7 : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  s7 (op) (x) (y) (p4) (p5) (p6) (p7) =
   let c = if op == 1 then x - y else 999
   in c

  m7 : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  m7 (op) (x) (y) (p4) (p5) (p6) (p7) =
   let c = if op == 1 then x * y else 999
   in c

  n7 : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  n7 (op) (x) (y) (p4) (p5) (p6) (p7) =
   let c = if op == 1 then (if x >= y then x + y else x) else 999
   in c

  chain : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  chain (op) (x) (y) (p4) (p5) (p6) (p7) =
   let c = if op == 1 then x + y
    else if op == 2 then x - y
    else if op == 3 then x * y
    else (if x >= y then x else y)
   in c

Section: Main

  opening : [Console] Nothing
  opening = act
   print-line-uni (show (w6 1 3 2 0 0 0))
   print-line-uni (show (w7 1 3 2 0 0 0 0))
   print-line-uni (show (w8 1 3 2 0 0 0 0 0))
   print-line-uni (show (w9 1 3 2 0 0 0 0 0 0))
   print-line-uni (show (s7 1 3 2 0 0 0 0))
   print-line-uni (show (m7 1 3 2 0 0 0 0))
   print-line-uni (show (n7 1 3 2 0 0 0 0))
   print-line-uni (show (chain 1 3 2 0 0 0 0))
   print-line-uni (show (chain 2 3 2 0 0 0 0))
   print-line-uni (show (chain 3 3 2 0 0 0 0))
   print-line-uni (show (chain 9 3 2 0 0 0 0))
  end
